      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2015 The Acta Anaesthesiologica Scandinavica Foundation. Published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Epidural catheters that are placed for post-operative analgesia have a significant failure rate in the first 24 hours. Beginning in 2011, we have used fluoroscopic guidance to place all non-obstetrical epidural catheters. In this retrospective analysis, we hypothesized that the characteristics of dye distribution on an epidurogram obtained immediately after catheter placement would predict clinical catheter function after surgery."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "The epidurograms and medical records of 303 consecutive patients who had epidural catheters placed for post-operative analgesia were reviewed. We extracted data on epidural dye distribution on the epidurograms and compared these results to the clinical function of the epidural catheters assessed on post-operative day 1 (POD1)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The three-dimensional pattern of epidural dye distribution (cephalad-caudad, right-left, anterior-posterior) had significant correlations with clinical function of an epidural catheter after surgery. Increased cephalad-caudad and anterior dye spread both correlated with decreased epidural solution infusion rates on POD1, whereas right- or left-sided dye distribution correlated with unilateral sensory deficits. A higher catheter placement on the neuraxis correlated with lower pain scores after thoracic surgery."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "An epidurogram obtained immediately after epidural catheter placement may have clinical utility for predicting clinical function of the catheter after surgery."
          }
        ]
      },
